// Verification half of the netflow node's record_integrity lane: read
// the last 90 minutes of this node's one archived class back (table-mode
// from_clickhouse — the vendor catch-all reconstruction), recompute
// each record's attestation fingerprint over the canonical
// serialization, and walk the per-node prev_event chain (chain_uid =
// tenzir-node-netflow); a Detection Finding (2004) drops for every
// break — content tamper (fingerprint_mismatch), deletion
// (chain_link_mismatch), or splice — while the windowed walk
// tolerates its own edge (the first record's predecessor lives
// outside the 90m read, its link checks are skipped, its fingerprint
// recompute is not).
//
// SCHEDULED, hourly — the every wrapper, the loader-pipeline shape
// (the core node's detection-attest-verify): the first walk fires at
// pipeline start and every 1h re-walks the rolling 90m window; every
// waits for a running walk before starting the next (no concurrent
// walks against the store); restart-on-error still relaunches the
// pipeline on a hard failure. Window dropped from 24h to 90m
// (tetragon parity, phase 2 stateless: the shorter window is the
// direct leg's traffic-light — if a chain breaks at 90m, the leg's
// straggler drop has been eating records for an hour; find the gap
// and the leak is contained).
//
// TWO legs out per break, the core lane's shape: stdout (pod-log
// visibility) and an ocsf_derive + ocsf_cast-dressed INSERT into
// ocsf.Detection_Finding with the tenzir user — the same table the
// Grafana tenzir-findings rule reads, so class 2004 + severity 4 land
// inside the rule's WHERE clauses and tamper evidence pages through
// the agent_invoke bridge. The written findings carry no attestation
// and this walk never reads ocsf.Detection_Finding back — the walk
// covers only the archive-rule class — so the lane never self-feeds.
// While a break persists, each hourly walk re-emits its finding
// (append-only evidence) and the rule re-pages until the tamper is
// remediated.
//
// ocsf.Network_Activity is the SHARED table: the tetragon node's
// archive rule writes its Network Activity rows here too, and this
// node's walk reads the whole table window — the verify operator
// walks each chain_uid separately, so the tetragon chain rides this
// walk as a second witness (and the tetragon node's own walk returns
// the favor). A netflow-side break names chain tenzir-node-netflow in
// the finding; a tetragon-chain break surfacing here names
// tenzir-node-tetragon — the chain_uid is the finding's scope, not a
// duplicate-page bug.
//
// SCOPE: this lane reads only this node's chain (the chain_uid filter
// below); the tetragon lane's cross-class 4001 rows in this shared
// table are walked by the tetragon lane, not here. At probe flow
// volume the shared read is negligible; if the UniFi exporter fix
// ever floods the class, the window is the operator's dial.
every 1h {
  from_clickhouse table="ocsf.Network_Activity",
    host="clickhouse-tenzir.tenzir.svc.cluster.local",
    port=9000,
    user="tenzir",
    password=secret("clickhouse-password"),
    tls=false
  where time > now() - 90m
  // Scope to THIS node's chain: ocsf.Network_Activity is shared — the
  // tetragon chain is cross-class (1007/1001/4001) and its 4001 rows
  // live here too; reading them back alongside this node's chain
  // breaks the table-local predecessor walk on healthy records (the
  // review's false-flag finding). The netflow chain alone is
  // table-contiguous.
  where attestation_list[0].chain_uid == "tenzir-node-netflow"
  homelab::record_integrity::verify product="homelab-detection",
    title="Detection record chain break",
    uid_prefix="detection",
    scope_word="chain",
    windowed=true,
    link="stream"
  fork {
    to_stdout { write_json }
  }
  ocsf_derive
  ocsf_cast
  to_clickhouse table="ocsf.Detection_Finding",
    host="clickhouse-tenzir.tenzir.svc.cluster.local",
    port=9000,
    user="tenzir",
    password=secret("clickhouse-password"),
    tls=false,
    mode="append",
    max_batch_rows=65536
}